--TEST--
an expression `catch` produces the arm's value where the guard threw and the guard's own where it did not — `rule:expressions/catch-expression`, `rule:expressions/catch-result-type` and `rule:expressions/catch-lowers-to-block-form`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:statements/catch-as-an-expression
// `rule:expressions/catch-lowers-to-block-form`: the guarded expression runs inside a protected region and each
// arm is a landing pad over it, so the whole expression produces either the
// guard's value or the matching arm's — one phi, never a statement in between.
class Repo {
    public static function get(int $id): int {
        if ($id < 0) {
            throw new IOError("no such row");
        }
        return $id * 2;
    }
}

// The guard completes: its own value, and no arm runs.
echo Repo::get(21) catch (IOError $missing) => 0, "\n";
// The guard throws: the matching arm's value.
echo Repo::get(-1) catch (IOError $missing) => 0, "\n";
// § 1: the binding is the same kind of name a clause's `$e` is, and carries
// the exception the guard threw.
echo Repo::get(-1) catch (IOError $e) => $e->message, "\n";
// § 1: the variable is optional, as it is in the block form.
echo Repo::get(-1) catch (IOError) => -7, "\n";
// § 4: the result is the union of the two sides, so a guarded `int` beside a
// `string` arm is what a `mixed` binding takes.
mixed $either = Repo::get(-1) catch (IOError $why) => "fell back";
echo $either, "\n";
--EXPECT--
42
0
no such row
-7
fell back
